.TH INVF-ROLLBACK 8 "2026-09" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
invf-rollback \- undo the last InvariantFS sweep from its checkpoint
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B invf-rollback
.I image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B invf-rollback
rolls a volume back to the savepoint taken by the last
.BR invf-sweep (8)
run. The savepoint (an
.B SPT0
descriptor at block-0 offset
.BR 0xA00 )
records the base-tree root and the delta end offset as they stood at
sweep start; a restore republishes that root and truncates the Delta Log
to that end, so everything the sweep appended past it vanishes wholesale
and the pre-sweep generation becomes the whole volume again. The result
is bit-exact the pre-sweep state.
.PP
This is an offline tool: the volume must not be mounted. The phases are
idempotent; a killed run simply continues when re-run.
.PP
The rollback window is always the
.I last
sweep: a new sweep drops the previous savepoint after capturing its own.
Use
.BR "invf-sweep \-\-realize"
to accept a sweep without running a new one. A sweep that never armed one
(arm refused) has nothing to roll back to, and this tool says so.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Rolled back.
.TP
1
No savepoint: nothing to roll back.
.TP
2
Refused: a live redundancy seal would be invalidated. Free it first with
.BR "invf-sweep \-\-free\-redundant" .
.TP
3
The savepoint descriptor failed verification; the post-sweep state is
untouched.
.TP
5
I/O or internal error; re-run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-sweep (8),
.BR invf-fsck (8),
.BR invarifs (7)
